This paper presents a geometric method for determining controlled invariant sets of hybrid systems. Building upon established notions of controlled invariant manifolds for smooth systems, the proposed approach extends geometric concepts to hybrid systems. Our method is versatile and can be seamlessly integrated into existing geometric tools for analyzing hybrid systems. A key advantage lies in the computational simplicity, as it leverages existing geometric solvers to calculate controlled invariant sets. The efficacy of this method is demonstrated through examples showcasing its utility. Furthermore, the paper highlights potential applications in synthesizing control strategies to enforce hybrid system constraints. Ultimately, the proposed geometric method provides a powerful framework for controlling hybrid systems. Its versatility and computational efficiency make it an attractive choice for a wide range of hybrid control problems. A companion MATLAB package is available to support the research findings. This paper is a companion study to \cite{Xia2021} and serves as a foundational work for a forthcoming paper on geometric control barrier functions. The accompanying code can be accessed at \cite{Xia2022}. The authors' corresponding email addresses are \texttt{xia@math.uvic.ca}, \texttt{liu.yuqiao@gmail.com}, \texttt{song.yuqiao@outlook.com} and \texttt{song.yuqiao@uvic.ca}. A preprint of this paper is available at \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.00123}. \vspace{-2mm} 
